Dear HR,
I am writing to raise a formal grievance concerning my established working arrangement and the proposed contractual variation that I have been asked to sign.
I have attempted to resolve my concerns informally through discussions and correspondence with my manager and HR. Although some amendments have been made to the original proposal, I remain concerned that the revised document does not accurately reflect my longstanding working arrangement and could introduce changes to my terms and conditions that I have not agreed to.
1. My established working arrangement since July 2022
Since joining the organisation as a Data Engineer in July 2022, I have worked predominantly from home, attending the London office approximately once per month for our monthly IT Team Day.
This arrangement was agreed with my manager when I joined and has remained consistent throughout my employment.
Importantly, I was still living in London for approximately the first three months of my employment and was already following this working pattern before moving to Sheffield. My manager was aware of my relocation, and my address was updated on the organisation's HR system.
For over four years, I have continued working under this arrangement without being advised that it was temporary, subject to a trial period or required reviewing every three months.
HR has also expressly acknowledged that this working pattern has existed since July 2022 and that it was informally agreed with my manager when I joined.
I therefore find it difficult to understand why this longstanding arrangement is now being treated as though it was only introduced in June 2026.
2. Failure to recognise the contractual significance of my arrangement
HR has stated that because my working arrangement was agreed informally and was not formally documented as a contractual variation, it does not form part of my contractual terms and conditions.
I do not believe sufficient consideration has been given to the fact that this arrangement was agreed at the start of my employment, consistently followed for over four years and accepted by management throughout that period.
I appreciate that my written contract identifies London as my usual place of work. However, my original employment documentation also expressly recognised that my working pattern included working from home.
I believe the organisation should properly consider whether the original agreement, together with the longstanding and consistent working practice, has contractual significance, including whether it has become an agreed or implied term.
I would therefore like HR to explain how it reached the conclusion that my arrangement has no contractual status despite the circumstances outlined above.
3. Proposed contractual variation
The original contractual letter issued in June 2026 described my working pattern as a new arrangement, effective from 2 June 2026, and subject to a temporary trial period.
Following my concerns, HR agreed to remove the references to the arrangement being temporary and subject to a trial.
However, the revised letter issued in August 2026 continues to describe my working arrangement as new and requires me to accept a variation to my employment contract.
This concerns me because I was not requesting a new working arrangement in June 2026. I was simply documenting the arrangement that had already been operating since July 2022, following a request from my manager.
HR has explained that the intention is to formalise my existing arrangement under the organisation's current hybrid-working framework.
If this is the case, I would like to understand why the document describes a new contractual change rather than accurately recording the arrangement already in place.
I am concerned about signing a document that could change my existing contractual position without fully understanding the implications.
4. Three-month review provision
One of my main concerns is the introduction of a requirement for my working arrangement to be reviewed every three months.
I understand that the organisation may need to review working arrangements from time to time to meet operational requirements.
However, my arrangement has operated successfully for over four years without a specific recurring three-month review requirement.
I am concerned that this new provision could potentially be used to increase my office attendance or otherwise change my established working pattern.
Although HR has stated that any future changes would be discussed with me, I would like clear confirmation that the three-month review does not give the organisation an automatic right to change my working arrangement without following the appropriate contractual process.
I would also like to understand why such frequent reviews are necessary in my circumstances.
5. Office attendance requirements
The revised contractual letter states that my working pattern is one office day per month.
However, HR's subsequent correspondence states that I may attend the office "at least one day per month".
I believe there is an important difference between these two descriptions.
My established arrangement has been to attend the office approximately once per month, ordinarily for the monthly IT Team Day.
I recognise that there may occasionally be genuine operational reasons for additional attendance. However, I am concerned that the revised wording could allow my office attendance to be increased without any clear limits or explanation.
I would therefore like confirmation of the actual office-attendance requirement I am being asked to accept, including when additional attendance could reasonably be required.
6. Contractual changes and consultation
I am also concerned that insufficient consideration has been given to the provisions in my existing employment contract governing changes to terms and conditions, particularly clause 19.6.
I would like HR to explain what contractual changes it considers are being introduced, whether these are regarded as minor or major changes, and what consultation or agreement process should apply.
If the organisation's intention is simply to document an existing arrangement, I do not understand why I am being asked to accept additional conditions that did not previously apply.
I believe I should be given a clear explanation of the contractual implications before being asked to sign.
7. Attempts to resolve the matter informally
I have made several attempts to resolve these concerns through discussions with my manager and HR, including the meeting held on 3 August 2026 and subsequent written correspondence.
Although I appreciate that HR removed some of the original wording, my main concerns remain unresolved.
I am particularly disappointed that HR has maintained its position without, in my view, adequately addressing the significance of the arrangement that has existed throughout my employment.
As HR has indicated that it does not intend to make further amendments, I feel I have no alternative but to raise these concerns formally.
8. Resolution sought
I would like this grievance to be resolved constructively. I am not seeking to prevent the organisation from reasonably managing its operational requirements, but I do want my existing working arrangement to be properly recognised and protected.
I am therefore seeking:
1.	Formal recognition that my working arrangement has been in place since July 2022, with management's knowledge and agreement.
2.	A proper explanation of why HR considers that this longstanding arrangement does not form part of my contractual terms, including consideration of whether it constitutes an express or implied term.
3.	An amended contractual document that accurately reflects my established working arrangement rather than describing it as a new arrangement introduced in June 2026.
4.	Clear confirmation of my office-attendance requirements, including the distinction between one day per month and at least one day per month.
5.	Clarification of the purpose and effect of the three-month review provision, including confirmation that it does not independently authorise changes to my contractual terms.
6.	Confirmation of the process that would be followed before any material changes to my working arrangement were proposed.
9. Current position and grievance hearing

Following my request for additional time to respond to the proposed contractual variation, HR agreed to extend the deadline to 13 October 2026.

Given that I am now raising a formal grievance, I request that this deadline be extended until the grievance has been concluded and I have had a reasonable opportunity to consider the outcome.
For clarity, I have not signed or accepted the proposed contractual variation, and I wish to continue working under my existing arrangement while these concerns are addressed.
I remain fully committed to my role and hope that this matter can be resolved fairly and constructively.

Please acknowledge this as a formal grievance and arrange a hearing in accordance with the organisation's grievance procedure. I would like to be accompanied by my trade union representative from The Representation Union.


